---
import TableScroll from './TableScroll.astro';
import SourceList from './SourceList.astro';
import { elfEntryPath } from '../data/manual-structure';
---
<section aria-labelledby="elf-startup">
  <h2 id="elf-startup" class="prm-section-heading ruled">ELF startup and memory in pTOS</h2>
  <p>Use <a href="/reference/pexecve/">Pexecve(0)</a> to load and run a native ELF executable with explicit vectors.
    Link libcmini’s <code>elfcrt0.o</code> for static ELF vector entry. On classic 68k with a shared address space,
    <code>CONF_WITH_M68K_SHARED_EXEC</code> enables <code>Pexecve(0)</code> for the configured PRG or ELF loader.
    On 68k, <a href="/reference/pexec/">Pexec(0)</a> also loads ELF when enabled, and <code>Pexec(3)</code> rejects staged ELF with <code>EPLFMT</code>.</p>
  <TableScroll label="ELF image placement and startup">
    <table class="prm-rule-table">
      <caption>ELF image placement and startup</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Launch path</th><th scope="col">Memory placement</th><th scope="col">Initial entry</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Static ELF, single address space</th>
          <td class="table-prose">Load segments into the TPA following the basepage, with padding where alignment requires it.
            Standard ELF entry reserves a 256 KiB stack area in the child’s TPA, which holds the initial vectors.</td>
          <td class="table-prose"><code>Pexecve(0)</code> on shared ARM and classic 68k uses the standard ELF stack.
            Classic 68k <code>Pexec(0)</code> uses it for <a href={`${elfEntryPath}#entry-note`}>marked ELF</a> with
            <code>CONF_WITH_M68K_SHARED_EXEC</code>. Unmarked images keep basepage entry.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Static ELF, private address space</th>
          <td class="table-prose">Load segments directly into the process’s private pages. The TPA holds the basepage. The image,
            startup storage and user stack occupy their own ranges.</td>
          <td>Vector launch enters the executable at <code>AT_ENTRY</code> with the stack pointer at <code>argc</code>.
            Private 68k <code>Pexec</code> modes 0, 100 and 200 derive the same ELF entry vectors from the classic tail and environment.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">ELF with <code>PT_INTERP</code></th>
          <td class="table-prose">Map the executable, interpreter and initial stack into the child’s private address space.
            The interpreter resolves shared-library dependencies before it transfers control to the executable.</td>
          <td class="table-prose">The interpreter starts with its stack pointer at <code>argc</code>, followed by <code>argv</code>,
            <code>envp</code> and the auxiliary vector. See <a href={elfEntryPath}>ELF32 process entry</a>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>At standard ELF entry, use <code>AT_PTOS_BASEPAGE</code> to find the basepage. Classic programs and accessories use
    the <a href={`${elfEntryPath}#basepage-entry`}>basepage entry convention</a>.
    Shared-address-space kernel AES can give marked ELF accessories the same <a href={`${elfEntryPath}#startup-role`}>standard stack</a>
    with the accessory role.</p>
  <p>For static ELF, <code>p_tbase</code> holds the relocated entry address and <code>p_dlen</code> is zero.
    The ELF loader reports the file-backed image through <code>p_tlen</code> and the trailing region through
    <code>p_bbase</code> and <code>p_blen</code>. Use the <code>PT_LOAD</code> records for individual segment boundaries.</p>
  <p>The classic TPA shrink example below applies to a 68k PRG. If its startup code reserves the stack in BSS,
    <code>p_blen</code> already includes those bytes.
    See also <a href="/subsystems/gemdos/the-program-format/#elf-executables">The program format</a>.</p>
</section>
